{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199 a period marked by political maneuvering military campaigns and significant territorial expansion. He was a prominent figure in the Crusades a key player in the complex relationships between European powers and the Islamic world. His reign witnessed both successes and challenges shaping the destiny of the Crusader states in the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond III was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were dominated by consolidating his rule and dealing with internal factions within the county. He faced opposition from various nobles and sought to assert his authority through a combination of diplomacy and military force. He also navigated the shifting alliances amongst the Crusader states and the Byzantine Empire. Raymond II's death left the county vulnerable and Raymond III had to quickly establish his legitimacy and secure his position as ruler.\n\nRaymond III's reign was characterized by extensive military activity. He engaged in numerous campaigns against Muslim forces seeking to expand the County's territory and secure its borders. He fought against the Zengi ruler of Mosul and Aleppo a formidable opponent who posed a constant threat to Tripoli's independence. Raymond III also participated in Crusades including the Third Crusade where he played a significant role in the defense of Jerusalem. His military successes significantly strengthened Tripoli's position and enhanced its prestige among the Crusader states. He formed alliances with other Crusader leaders and contributed troops to various campaigns.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* Raymond III was a skilled diplomat adept at forging alliances and navigating complex political landscapes. He maintained relationships with various powers including the Holy Roman Empire the Kingdom of Sicily and the Byzantine Empire. He sought to balance his relationships to secure Tripoli's interests and prevent it from being isolated. His diplomatic efforts often involved strategic marriages and political agreements. He understood the importance of alliances in maintaining stability and projecting power in the region.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy* In his later years Raymond III continued to face challenges from Muslim rulers and internal dissent. He focused on strengthening Tripoli's defenses and consolidating its economic base. He died in 1199 leaving the County of Tripoli to his son Raymond IV. Raymond III's reign is remembered as a period of significant growth and prosperity for Tripoli. He expanded the county's territory strengthened its military and enhanced its diplomatic standing. His legacy as a capable ruler and a key figure in the Crusades endures. He left behind a more secure and powerful county than the one he inherited solidifying Tripoli's place in the history of the Crusader states. His efforts to maintain independence in a volatile region were crucial to the survival of the county for decades to come.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0017849955474957824}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2533552050590515}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.2717786729335785}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.06198554113507271}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0002470903273206204}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.6464111215318553e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.510157047363464e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.4318398573086597e-05}, {"token": "4",